# THE LOCAL DEPLOYMENT — Caddy in front of the app's Docker image, on this machine.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Next.js's own self-hosting guide says to put a reverse
# proxy in front of the app rather than exposing its server to the network:
# the proxy absorbs malformed requests, slow-connection attacks, payload limits
# and rate limiting, so the app spends its resources rendering. On Vercel that
# was somebody else's job. From here it is ours, so it gets a checked-in file
# rather than a command somebody once typed. What both Caddyfiles share lives
# in common.caddy; this file holds only what is local.
#
# WHAT IT PROVES BEFORE A SERVER EXISTS. Three things in this repo depend on
# being behind an HTTPS proxy and cannot be checked by any unit test:
#
#   1. `src/db/authCookies.ts` decides the session cookie's `Secure` flag from
#      `x-forwarded-proto`. Its own comment says "Vercel always sets it", and a
#      proxy that does not sets the session cookie WITHOUT `Secure` on every
#      refresh — silently. Caddy sends `x-forwarded-proto: https`, and
#      /api/healthz reports what the app concluded as `proto`, which
#      infra/deploy.sh checks through this proxy after every deploy.
#   2. The app must not be reachable except through this proxy. The app
#      container publishes no port (infra/compose.yaml); Caddy reaches it as
#      app:3000 on the compose network.
#   3. Auth redirects are built from `window.location.origin`, so a new origin
#      has to be on Supabase's redirect allow-list or sign-up and password
#      reset bounce. That is a dashboard change, listed as blocker
#      B-SUPABASE-REDIRECT in Plans/caddy-deploy-and-cicd.md.
#
# NO HSTS HERE, AND IT IS NOT AN OVERSIGHT. Strict-Transport-Security applies
# to a HOST and ignores the port, so an HSTS header served for `localhost`
# would make the browser upgrade `http://localhost:3000` to https as well —
# and :3000 is the owner's `next dev`, which has no certificate. One header
# here would break the daily driver in a way that looks like a dead dev
# server. HSTS belongs in the production Caddyfile, on a real domain, and
# nowhere near localhost.

{
	# No admin API. It needs no authentication by design and binds :2019; on a
	# box this is a local privilege path, and here it is simply not needed.
	admin off

	# Caddy's own CA, because there is no public DNS name yet and ACME cannot
	# issue for `localhost`. The root has to be trusted once by hand — see
	# infra/README.md. Caddyfile.production uses Let's Encrypt instead.
	local_certs
}

# Both names are served because they resolve differently and the browser is on
# the other side of WSL: `localhost` is in /etc/hosts as 127.0.0.1, while
# `daygame.localhost` resolves to ::1 through the stub resolver and is not
# guaranteed to resolve from Windows at all. Serving both means whichever one
# answers in the browser is already configured.
localhost, daygame.localhost {
	import /etc/caddy/common.caddy
}
